 "asked_at": "2026-10-18T00:45+08:00",
 "answer": "The air quality is currently significantly better in the **north** than in the **west** of Singapore, though both regions are experiencing unhealthy haze conditions. \n\nAccording to the latest National Environment Agency (NEA) readings:\n\n* **North Singapore:** Has the lowest pollution levels on the island right now, with the **24-hour PSI around 129–131** (\"Unhealthy\" range) and lower 1-hour PM2.5 concentrations (hovering in the Elevated to low-High band, around 90–125 µg/m³).\n* **West Singapore:** Is currently the worst-hit region on the island. The **24-hour PSI has climbed above 200 (202–212)**, pushing it into the **\"Very Unhealthy\"** range, while 1-hour PM2.5 readings have remained in the High band (around 185–200 µg/m³).\n\n**Health Advisory:** \nEven though the north is faring better than the west, air quality across all of Singapore remains unhealthy due to regional smoke haze. Residents in both regions—especially children, the elderly, pregnant women, and those with respiratory or heart conditions—are advised to minimize or avoid prolonged and strenuous outdoor activities.",
